Bug: a header a parse found before the removal saw it keeps old references after a change

ADR-0036 says a file the server has not seen before has changed. The removal
(`removeReferencesOfChangedFiles()`, `src/rebuild.c`) instead records the token of a
file seen for the first time and keeps its references. A header reached only through
`<...>`, which the scan does not record, is first known from a parse, after that
request's removal has run. If it changes on disk before the next request, the removal
sees the new content first, keeps the parse's old references, and notices nothing, so
here `p.h:1` is left as a ghost. With `#include "p.h"` the scan finds it in the first
request and the change is seen (a control run on WSL, session d25255b8, 2026-11-18).

The likely fix: a file seen for the first time that has references has changed, as
the definition says, i.e. it loses them and the change is noticed. Decide first what
`records_the_token_of_a_header_seen_for_the_first_time_and_keeps_its_references`
(`src/rebuild_tests.c`) should become, since it pins today's behaviour, and that a cold
start, where every file is first seen before any parse, stays cheap. Backlog #112.
